content.text:
Time negative? Interesting; time initial -0.376? Probably RAF timing issue. Not important.

Now balance simulation. We'll write an eval that:
- pauses the RAF? The loop is always running. We can set `game.paused = true` to stop update, then manually set paused=false? update() checks `!this.paused && !this.over || this.observing`. If paused true, no world updates, but calling game.update(dt) still won't update due to paused true. Set paused=false.

To prevent interference from RAF during eval (JS single-threaded, eval blocks RAF), fine.

Simulation strategy:
1. Select all French units (including artillery?) and order them to move to attack positions.
2. Issue attack on a central British unit (maybe Foot Guards) so all move toward, then they auto-acquire nearest.
3. Run in chunks, every 10s of game time, re-order: move to farm zone (1435,842) perhaps for infantry, artillery hold back at 1200,1000 and set target. Actually player has all units selected by default? Initial selects playerUnits[1]. For easiest "skilled player" use all.
4. detect game.over.

But we want to validate balance, not cheat. Using all units and move to farm seems plausible.

Script:
```
const g=window.game;
if (g.over) return 'already over';
g.paused=false;
g.selection.clear();
g.playerUnits.forEach(u=>{if(!u.dead&&!u.routing)g.selection.add(u)});
g.refreshSelection();
const farm=g.terrain.farmZone;
// artillery target first british
...
for (let step=0; step<240; step++) { // 240 * 5s = 1200s game
  if (step%2===0) { // every 10s orders
     const enemy = g.units.find(u=>u.side==='gb' && !u.dead && !u.routing && u.short==='Foot Guards') || ...
     g.issueAttack(enemy)
  }
  for (let i=0;i<100;i++){ g.update(0.05); if(g.over) break; }
  if(g.over) break;
}
return {...}
```
But `issueAttack` adds rings/messages each 10s; fine.
However updating 100 steps of .05 = 5s. 240*100 = 24000 updates. Each update loops 13 units and ~350 soldiers plus ragdolls, likely okay maybe 5-20s. Let's do 150 steps = 750s at first; battle should be decided within a few minutes of game time. But smoke particles and DOM messages (setTimeout didn't run during eval) could accumulate; messages remove via timers only after eval, but DOM children cap 4. Timers game.after accumulate? Updated every loop. Fine.

One issue: game.over triggers endBattle, which manipulates DOM and audio bugle, okay.

After simulation, results. But this will alter visual page. We'll reload after.

Let's run a simulation, collect snapshots of morale/positions at intervals to reason.

